cord highs and lows, by the decade that set them","p_rec":"If Fort Wayne's climate weren't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, Fort Wayne has set <strong>45 standing record lows</strong>, fewer than the 77 that chance alone would give, and <strong>91 record highs</strong>, close to the 77 that chance alone would give. The 2010s hold the most record highs of any decade (50). Fort Wayne's all-time high is 106\u00b0F, on July 22, 1934.","t_chg":"Fort Wayne's nights have warmed since 1986. Its days have warmed.","p_chg":"Across 121 complete years: Fort Wayne's average low shows no clear change: +0.07\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.01 to +0.15) that includes zero. It's close, but the range still includes zero. Fort Wayne's average high has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.13\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.04 to +0.22), about +1.6\u00b0F since 1899. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1986, lows have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.41\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.02 to +0.81) and highs have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.47\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.03 to +0.91). The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 2 of 12 months. Highs rose in 3 of 12 months.","t_rain":"Fort Wayne has gotten wetter","p_rain":"Fort Wayne's yearly precipitation has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.53 inches per decade</strong> (95% range +0.23 to +0.82), about +6.6 inches since 1899. The 1991\u20132020 normal is 39.5 inches a year. The wettest year was 1990 (54.58 in); the driest, 2025 (24.31 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":false,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 6 daily record highs, 1 daily record low"}}